Attach the CPU interrupt controller before registering it

IOCPUInterruptController is created and initialised by a kext and then handed
to registerCPUInterruptController(), but nothing ever attaches it.  Its
parent chain on the service plane is empty, so registerService() throws it
away as a non member -- that is the "not registry member" line, and the trace
added earlier prints the same object as S1 IOCPUInterruptController up=1
p=0, one hop and then NULL.

Everything that later asks the platform expert for the interrupt controller
therefore finds nothing.  Attach it first, to the platform expert: that is
the object which adopts it on the very next line anyway, so the only
behaviour this changes is that the registry finally holds it.

attach(NULL) would replace the service root, hence the pe non NULL guard.

AT prints on attach, AT cpuic fail prints the platform pointer if attach is
refused or the platform expert does not exist yet.

---

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOCPU.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOCPU.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOCPU.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOCPU.cpp
@@ -590,6 +590,21 @@ IOCPUInterruptController::initCPUInterruptController(int sources, int cpus)
 void
 IOCPUInterruptController::registerCPUInterruptController(void)
 {
+	/* nio: nothing in this tree attaches this controller, so it has no
+	 * parent in the service plane and registerService() throws it away as
+	 * a non member.  Every client that later looks the platform interrupt
+	 * controller up then waits forever.  Attach it to the platform expert
+	 * first: that is the object which adopts it on the next line anyway.
+	 * attach(NULL) is not an option, it would replace the service root. */
+	IOPlatformExpert * pe = getPlatform();
+
+	if (getProvider() == NULL) {
+		if (pe && attach(pe)) {
+			IOLog("nio: AT cpuic");
+		} else {
+			IOLog("nio: AT cpuic fail pe=%p", pe);
+		}
+	}
 	setProperty(gPlatformInterruptControllerName, kOSBooleanTrue);
 	registerService();
 
